    "about": {
      "type": "chapter-about",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "ABOUT THIS CHAPTER",
        "show_warp_icon": true,
        "katha": "Varanasi has woven silk and gold for centuries, and its looms still set the standard for the Indian wedding saree. This chapter pairs that weight and richness with its opposite: light chiffon, the sheer, floating cloth that carries the same gold borders at a fraction of the heaviness.",
        "about": "North India is where the Mughal courts met the looms of Varanasi, and where textile design took on its enduring vocabulary of paisley, vine and trellis. Banaras weaving is the region’s great tradition: dense, luminous and made to be inherited. Chiffon sits next to it as a lighter way into the same palette, a fabric of fine, twisted yarn that moves in the air and takes a woven border beautifully.",
