    "landscape": {
      "type": "chapter-landscape",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "The landscape behind the loom",
        "paragraph_1": "Varanasi sits on the Ganga, and its weaving quarters have long clustered in the lanes behind the ghats. Silk came upriver, gold and silver thread came from specialist workshops, and designs followed the tastes of courts and temples.",
        "paragraph_2": "Courtly demand shaped the cloth. Mughal patrons favoured flowers, vines and trellis patterns, and Banaras weavers turned those into the jaal, the all-over lattice that is still the region’s signature.",
        "paragraph_3": "Chiffon is a different kind of cloth: a modern, lightweight fabric rather than a regional craft. We place it in this chapter because it pairs most naturally with Banaras, sharing its gold-edged borders and festive palette in a lighter weight.",
        "image_position_mobile": "below_text",
        "background_color": "#ffffff",
